 a  tempo back it up a little bit and we can  see how even this motorcycle pulling  around the corner feels in sync with the  pedestrian turning around i want to be  clear adherence to this tempo is more  jazz than electronic drum machine and i  doubt the scenes are actually executed  using a metronome but in the same way  musicians work together to keep the time  in a song i think wes is having his  actors extras and camera operators work  together to roughly keep the time of the  scene something which is then reinforced  in editing once a rhythm is established  west can change it or break it to match  shifts in emotion or for comedic effect  yes it is  no it isn't yes it is noise yes it is no  it is it is yes it is all artists sell  all their work it's what makes you an  artist selling it it's pretty astounding  how intricate this choreography is often  the turn of an actor's head or the  glance of their eyes is precisely timed  and there are shots like this one where  even this extras barely perceptible  blink is in time with the start of the  shot and the beat of the camera moving  this is a technique wes has used in the  past but he adheres to it more formally  in this film than ever before  in addition to the strict choreography  of the action he also heightens his  style by using other methods to abstract  elements of the story even more than he  normally would in the grand budapest  hotel wes would occasionally shift into  a visually absurd space but the  stylization still happened mostly in set  design choreography and performance the  action of that film still felt like it  was taking place in a physically real  space with real people walking through  it for the most part in the french  dispatch the action becomes more  abstract characters frequently stare  directly into the camera are frozen in  time or in a pose sometimes only a few  frozen as if they're posing for a  photograph while the rest of the world  moves around them  the geography of the set sometimes  doesn't make sense this depiction of  this room and this depiction don't line  up spatially from shot to shot  and then suddenly in a spike of  intensity the film will slip into  momentary naturalism complete with  handheld camera work  only to snap back to its stiff  abstraction moments later  lighting changes dramatically around the  characters characters speak to each  other in different languages without  translation wes mixes miniature and live  action photography in the same shots he  uses stop motion and 2d animation  wherever it suits him lights exterior  locations to make them look even more  like sets switches frequently between  black and white and color photography  and often changes aspect ratio even for  a single shot within a scene all of this  pushes the film towards a point of  almost absurd surrealism but does it  have a purpose  wes is clearly having fun and doing  things his own way but is that all he's  doing or does drawing our attention back  to artifice style and the act of  storytelling actually serve a purpose in  this story  his writers line the spines of every  good american library  the film is a very thinly disguised  tribute to the new yorker a magazine wes  anderson has collected and been obsessed  with since childhood not only is arthur  howitzer jr the editor of the fictional  magazine played by bill murray based on  elements from actual editors at the new  yorker but anderson draws heavy  inspiration from specific articles from  the magazine and the style of the  writers who wrote them i've already  written a thousand words take for  example the middle story which is almost  a direct adaptation of parts of mavis  gallant's article the events in may a  paris notebook part 1. the events  depicted in this story mirror some of  those accounted in the article the  touching narcissism of the young but  kremens herself is clearly modeled after  gallant in certain ways her writing  style is borrowed from gland attack in a  kind of 