ters wrote in a  unique way but they wrote in a way that  felt completely and entirely their own  their writing uses rhythm tangents  shifts in perspective abstraction and  all other manner of poetic and stylistic  license it's a designation that i'd have  no trouble applying to wes anderson's  own work and perhaps these writers were  in part the influences that gave him the  confidence to work in a way that was so  entirely his own the french dispatch  celebrates the joy of stylistic excess  of having fun with storytelling and  showcasing your creative personality  even in the midst of telling serious  stories poetic not necessarily in a bad  way oh while the film itself does these  things the film stylistically is an  expression of the unique voices of each  writer and their playfulness with style  and tone the film exists in a surrealist  space but unlike many forms of  surrealist filmmaking not a dream like  one instead it's an imaginative one  almost as if the film exists in the  minds of the writers writing the stories  or in anderson's as he reads them and so  the film visually comes to life not as  an attempt to translate its source  material into realism and then depict  that as many film adaptations of writing  do but it brings the prose it mimics to  life in a way that reflects the joy of  poetic license  shots like this are not just poetic in  how they're written but in how the scene  is visually constructed and  choreographed writing has a flexibility  to shift perspective skip definitely  through time freeze time and examine a  moment shift in style and tone and  create abstraction and film shares all  these possibilities but normal  conventions of filmmaking don't  generally allow for them to be employed  or if they are it's generally one or two  not everything in the kitchen sink  tossed into a single film but if you're  going to do that what better place to do  it than in a film about a magazine that  allowed its writers to flex their  stylistic muscles and to be totally  themselves as artists you see the girl  on it no  trust me she's there when we reduce how  we think about films or any art merely  to the content to what it exposes we can  lose out on the pleasure they can hold  in other ways the beauty of a shot or  how a scene is constructed sometimes is  in my opinion enough the point is he  could paint this beautifully if he  wanted but he thinks this was better i  think i sort of agree with him  sometimes the rhyme and rhythm of a poem  is enjoyable even when you don't  understand what it means  and not all music needs lyrics  in the french dispatch within each story  there are moments where the black and  white photography erupts into color  these shifts delineate time helping keep  separate the film's many parentheticals  and shifts in perspective but they also  help illuminate what i think was most  vivid for each storyteller in their  story for berenson it was the art itself  for crements it was the youth for  roebuck wright it was the food and  within the larger story not the articles  within but within the french dispatch  the film what's rendered in color is  what is most vivid for wes anderson the  storyteller  the writers in the act of storytelling  are what's portrayed in color it's easy  to imagine howitzer junior's advice  applying to wes's own films however you  do things just try to make it seem like  you shot it that way on purpose the  documentary obit about obituary writers  at the new york times is a great  documentary companion piece to the  french dispatch the guy who runs the  times clip archive even seems like he  could be a character out of a wes  anderson film you can watch this  documentary which i love right now on  curiosity stream which has thousands of  educational and documentary titles and  when you sign up for curiosity stream  you'll also get access to nebula a  streaming service built and owned by  creators like myself you'll get bonus  videos from me as well as all the