cles and to be totally  themselves as artists you see the girl  on it no  trust me she's there when we reduce how  we think about films or any art merely  to the content to what it exposes we can  lose out on the pleasure they can hold  in other ways the beauty of a shot or  how a scene is constructed sometimes is  in my opinion enough the point is he  could paint this beautifully if he  wanted but he thinks this was better i  think i sort of agree with him  sometimes the rhyme and rhythm of a poem  is enjoyable even when you don't  understand what it means  and not all music needs lyrics  in the french dispatch within each story  there are moments where the black and  white photography erupts into color  these shifts delineate time helping keep  separate the film's many parentheticals  and shifts in perspective but they also  help illuminate what i think was most  vivid for each storyteller in their  story for berenson it was the art itself  for crements it was the youth for  roebuck wright it was the food and  within the larger story not the articles  within but within the french dispatch  the film what's rendered in color is  what is most vivid for wes anderson the  storyteller  the writers in the act of storytelling  are what's portrayed in color it's easy  to imagine howitzer junior's advice  applying to wes's own films however you  do things just try to make it seem like  you shot it that way on purpose the  documentary obit about obituary writers  at the new york times is a great  documentary companion piece to the  french dispatch the guy who runs the  times clip archive even seems like he  could be a character out of a wes  anderson film you can watch this  documentary which i love right now on  curiosity stream which has thousands of  educational and documentary titles and  when you sign up for curiosity stream  you'll also get access to nebula a  streaming service built and owned by  creators like myself you'll get bonus  videos from me as well as all the